== Periodic modules <sec:periodic-modules>

#definition[
  A $k G$-module $M$ is *periodic*#idx("periodic module")#idx(
    "module",
    "periodic",
  ) if $Omega^n M tilde.equiv M$ for some value of $n$. The minimal value of $n$
  for which this is true is called the *period* of $M$.
] <def:periodic-module>

#proposition(title: [Carlson [@bib:Carlson1977]])[
  Suppose $M$ is a finitely generated indecomposable periodic $k G$-module of
  period $n$, with $k$ algebraically closed. Then the nilpotent elements in
  $Ext^*_(k G) (M, M)$ form an ideal, and the ring $Ext^*_(k G) (M, M)$ is a
  direct sum of this ideal and a polynomial subring $k[x]$ in one variable $x$
  of degree $n$.
] <prop:periodic-ext-ring>

#proof[
  Since $M$ is indecomposable, $End_(k G) (M)$ is a local ring, so that modulo
  its radical it is isomorphic to $k$. Let $x in Ext^n_(k G) (M, M)$ correspond
  to an isomorphism $hat(x) : Omega^n M -> M$. If $zeta in Ext^r_(k G) (M, M)$
  is any element of positive degree represented by a map
  $hat(zeta) : Omega^r M -> M$, then $hat(zeta)^n : Omega^(r n) M -> M$ is
  either an isomorphism or nilpotent. If it is an isomorphism then it differs
  from a multiple of $hat(x)^r$ by a nilpotent element.
]

#corollary[
  If $M$ is a finitely generated indecomposable periodic $k G$-module and $k$ is
  algebraically closed, then $V_G (M)$ is a single line through the origin in
  $V_G$.
] <cor:periodic-variety-line>

#proof[
  It follows from the proposition that $tilde(V)_G (M)$ is an affine line
  $AA^1 (k)$. Since $tilde(V)_G (M)$ is a finite cover of $V_G (M)$, the latter
  is also a single line through the origin.
]

Conversely, we have the following:

#theorem(title: [Eisenbud [@bib:Eisenbud1980]])[
  #source(204)If $M$ is a finitely generated $k G$-module of complexity one,
  then $M$ is a direct sum of periodic modules and projective modules.
] <th:eisenbud>
#idx("Eisenbud's theorem", target: [@th:eisenbud])#idx(
  "theorem",
  "Eisenbud's",
  target: [@th:eisenbud],
)

#proof[
  (Carlson [@bib:Carlson1983]) By
  Proposition~@prop:variety-dimension-complexity, if $M$ has complexity one then
  $V_G (M)$ is a finite union of lines through the origin in $V_G$. So we may
  choose a homogeneous element $zeta in H^n (G, k)$ such that
  $V_G chevron.l zeta chevron.r inter V_G (M) = {0}$. Tensor the sequence
  $ 0 -> L_zeta -> Omega^n k -> k -> 0 $
  with $M$ to obtain a sequence
  $ 0 -> L_zeta times.o M -> Omega^n k times.o M -> M -> 0. $
  By Proposition~@prop:l-zeta-variety and Theorem~@th:variety-tensor-product we
  have
  $
    V_G (L_zeta times.o M) = V_G (L_zeta) inter V_G (M)
    = V_G chevron.l zeta chevron.r inter V_G (M) = {0}
  $
  and so by Proposition~@prop:variety-dimension-complexity, $L_zeta times.o M$
  is projective, and hence also injective (Proposition~3.1.2 of Volume~I). Since
  $Omega^n k times.o M tilde.equiv Omega^n M plus.o ("projective")$, we have
  $ M tilde.equiv Omega^n M plus.o ("projective"). $
]

#definition[
  If $M$ is periodic, an element $zeta in H^n (G, k)$ with
  $V_G chevron.l zeta chevron.r inter V_G (M) = {0}$ is said to *generate the
  periodicity*#idx("generating", "periodicity")#idx(
    "periodicity",
    "generating",
  ) of $M$.
] <def:generate-periodicity>

The proof of the above theorem shows that if $zeta in H^n (G, k)$ generates the
periodicity of a periodic module $M$ then the period of $M$ divides $n$.

#corollary[
  If $H^* (G, k)$ is finitely generated as a module over a subring generated by
  elements $x_1, dots, x_s$ in degrees $n_1, dots, n_s$ and $M$ is an
  indecomposable finitely generated periodic $k G$-module, then the period of
  $M$ divides one of the $n_i$.
] <cor:period-divides-degree>

#proof[
  The condition on the $x_i$ ensures that the intersection of the hyperplanes
  they define is the origin. Now $V_G (M)$ is a single line through the origin
  by Corollary~@cor:periodic-variety-line, so one of the $x_i$ generates the
  periodicity of $M$.
]

#corollary[
  If $M$ is an indecomposable finitely generated periodic $k E$-module with $E$
  elementary abelian, then $M$ has period one or two. If $k$ has characteristic
  two, then $M$ has period one.
] <cor:elementary-abelian-period>

#proof[
  This follows from the structure of $H^* (E, k)$ given in Corollary~3.5.7 of
  Volume~I.
]
